<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Herat</h1><p class="st-meta">1818 CE – 1863 CE (approx.) · capital Herat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last outpost of Sadozai rule, a frontier city-state whose sieges by Persia drew Britain and Russia into the Great Game.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barakzai-dynasty">Barakzai</a> brothers took over most of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a> around 1818, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadozai">Sadozai</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-durrani">Mahmud Shah</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamran-shah-durrani">Kamran Shah</a> held on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, ruling it as an independent principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamran-shah-durrani">Kamran Shah's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yar-muhammad-khan-alakozai">Yar Muhammad Khan Alakozai</a> became the real power and in 1842 killed Kamran, ruling himself until 1851. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> was a walled city on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and its strategic value made it central to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>. Persia besieged it in 1837 to 1838, with a young British officer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eldred-pottinger">Eldred Pottinger</a>, helping the defence, and again in 1856, when <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> went to war with Persia to force its withdrawal.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the Treaty of Paris in 1857 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> renounced its claims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1863 after a ten-month siege, dying there days later, and Herat joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-herat.jpg" alt="Principality of Herat" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Herat_until_1842.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1818 CE – 1863 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Herat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kamran Shah, Yar Muhammad Khan Alakozai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Herat, Sadozai Herat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=principality-of-herat" aria-label="Principality of Herat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6262 934 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Herat lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6582 1190L6757 1183M6582 1190L6757 1183"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6757 1183h0M6757 1183h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6582 1190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Herat. <a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=principality-of-herat">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
